**Read:** [Engineering Statics — Baker and Haynes](../sources/Engineering-Statics-Baker-and-Haynes.lingua#r_3DXGKGQSPPA9YDNH2WSB8XRN80), chapters 1–3, with the vector and particle-equilibrium exercises. Revisit your component and assembly boundaries.

A force is an interaction, not a property stored in an isolated part. Contact, gravity, fluid pressure and electromagnetic actuation have different physical origins. The action/reaction pair acts on different bodies; both forces do not belong on the same isolated-body diagram merely because both exist.

An ideal cable transmits tension along its direction; a smooth unilateral contact pushes normal to its surface and cannot pull across an open gap. Real cables, bearings and contacts have additional behavior, but the idealization must be stated before it is used.

**Work:** Isolate a small hanging load, its connector and its support in three separate sketches. Place each interaction on the appropriate body. Explain whether a calculated negative force changes the assumed direction or violates a tension-only/contact-only assumption. Solve particle equilibrium in two and three dimensions, then state what the particle model omits.

